Bagsakan POS + Inventory | Developer Specification 1
BAGSAKAN POS + INVENTORY SYSTEM
Developer Flow & Functional Specification
Prepared for Worthy Acosta | October 2, 2026
Build a system for wholesale purchasing and retail selling. Maintain one SKU per product while
supporting multiple suppliers, purchase costs, receiving batches, selling units, and changing
selling prices.
Core rule: Purchase cost and selling price are separate records. Admin can access financial
information. Cashiers see selling prices and available stock only. Purchasing users can access
buying costs while selling and profit information can be restricted.
1. Overall system flow
Supplier → Purchase order / purchase entry
↓
Receiving → Batch / lot with supplier and purchase cost
↓
Inventory → Stock quantity, location and movements
↓
Pricing → Authorized SRP setting / update
↓
POS sale → Checkout and stock deduction
↓
Payment or receivable → Sales, inventory and financial reports
This document consolidates the requested system explanation. Amounts and transactions below
are illustrative examples.
Bagsakan POS + Inventory | Developer Specification 2
2. Products, suppliers and purchase costs
Product masterfile: SKU, product name, category, base inventory unit, purchase/selling units and
conversion factors, current default SRP, low-stock threshold, and active status.
Warehouse/location balances should be tracked separately.
A product keeps one SKU across suppliers. Each receipt creates its own batch with supplier,
receipt date, invoice/DR reference, quantity, unit cost and location. A new purchase must never
overwrite historical costs.
SKU: VEG-TOM-001 / Tomato Quantity Cost per kg Total cost
Supplier A / Batch 001 100 kg ₱55 ₱5,500
Supplier B / Batch 002 80 kg ₱60 ₱4,800
Supplier C / Batch 003 50 kg ₱52 ₱2,600
Total 230 kg - ₱12,900
Cashier view: Tomato | Available: 230 kg | SRP: ₱90/kg. No supplier, buying cost, average cost,
valuation or margin appears.
3. Costing method
Support FIFO or weighted average costing, with the chosen method controlled by Admin.
Weighted average is a practical starting option for this specification. Keep supplier and batch
costs even when average costing is used.
Example: ₱12,900 ÷ 230 kg = ₱56.09/kg rounded average cost. At SRP ₱90/kg, estimated gross
profit is ₱33.91/kg before expenses. Retain sufficient internal precision and round only displayed
amounts.
If all 230 kg sell at ₱90/kg: potential sales are ₱20,700 and potential gross profit is ₱7,800. These
are projections, not completed sales or net profit.
Use one consistent costing method for posted transactions. A method change needs a controlled
transition; it must not silently rewrite historical results. Physical batch allocation and financial
costing are separate concerns.
Bagsakan POS + Inventory | Developer Specification 3
4. User access and cost confidentiality
Information / action Admin / Owner Purchasing / Inventory Cashier
SKU and stock quantities Full Allowed Allowed
Suppliers, buying costs and purchase
history
Full Allowed Denied
SRP / selling information Full Optional restriction Allowed
Profit, valuation and financial reports Full Denied by default Denied
Receive goods / input buying costs Full Allowed Denied
Change SRP / discounts Full Only if authorized Approval required
Spoilage / stock adjustments Approve / post Submit; approve if
authorized
Denied by default
Users and audit logs Full Restricted Restricted
POS checkout / customer payment Full Only if authorized Allowed within
assigned duties
Admin / Owner: Manage purchasing, receiving, inventory, pricing, sales, expenses, receivables,
supplier payables, cash/bank balances, financial reports, users and approvals.
Purchasing / Inventory: See suppliers, purchase prices/history, quantities ordered/received and
warehouse locations. Hide selling prices, profits, total sales, balances and company financial
reports when configured by Admin.
Cashier: Search/scan products, see SRP and available quantity, enter sale quantity, complete
checkout and access permitted receipts. No purchase-price or profit access.
Backend requirement: Enforce role and user permissions on every API, report, export and print
route. Do not send sensitive cost data to restricted users and merely hide it on screen. Test
direct requests and unauthorized record access.
Permissions must be customizable per user. Audit information must also respect cost
confidentiality. A person who knows both purchase and selling prices may calculate a margin
themselves; permissions restrict system disclosure.
Bagsakan POS + Inventory | Developer Specification 4
5. Purchasing and receiving flow
Select supplier and existing SKU
↓
Enter quantity, purchase unit and purchase price
↓
Record purchase date, invoice / DR number and payment terms
↓
Receive actual quantity into selected warehouse / location
↓
Create receiving batch and post inventory movement
↓
Increase available stock and update costing / supplier balance
Example: ABC Vegetable Trading | DR 10558 | October 2, 2026 | Tomato: 150 kg × ₱58/kg =
₱8,700. Confirmed receipt increases stock by 150 kg.
Separate ordered quantity from received quantity. An order alone does not increase stock.
Record partial receipts, shortages and purchase returns against their source documents.
6. Wholesale purchases and retail units
Use one base inventory unit with defined conversion factors. Support fractional kg for weighed
products and reject invalid unit conversions.
Eggs example Inventory effect / price
Purchase 10 trays; 1 tray = 30 pcs Receive 300 pcs
Sell 2 trays Deduct 60 pcs
Sell 5 individual eggs Deduct 5 pcs; remaining stock = 235 pcs
Example retail price choices 1 pc ₱8; 6 pcs ₱45; 12 pcs ₱85; 1 tray ₱190
Store the unit, conversion factor and price used on each transaction so later changes do not alter
existing documents.
Bagsakan POS + Inventory | Developer Specification 5
7. POS sales and flexible selling prices
Log in → Search or scan product
↓
Enter quantity / selling unit → Display authorized SRP
↓
Add to cart → Apply approved discount or price override
↓
Checkout → Select payment method and customer if needed
↓
Post sale → Print / send receipt and deduct stock once
Cashier screen: product name, SKU, available stock, SRP, quantity, approved discount and line
total. Purchase data is excluded.
Flexible pricing: Store a default SRP, but allow Admin or an authorized supervisor to update it or
approve a transaction-specific override. Cashier overrides are disabled by default. Save the
actual selling price on every sale line.
Tomato selling-price history SRP per kg
October 1 ₱85
October 2 ₱90
October 3 ₱95
October 4 ₱88
Keep purchase-cost history and selling-price history separately, including date/time, user,
old/new values and reason. Historical prices must remain available to authorized users.
8. Credit sales and collections
Payment choices: cash, GCash, bank transfer, credit and mixed payment. A credit sale requires a
customer, invoice, terms and due date. Cashiers receive only the customer-balance information
explicitly authorized for checkout.
Example: Sentro 88 Foods Inc. | SI-00158 | ₱15,800 | Invoice date October 3, 2026 | 30-day term
| Due November 2, 2026. A ₱5,000 collection reduces the outstanding amount to ₱10,800.
Allocate payments to invoices and record payment date/method/reference. A later collection
increases cash/bank and reduces receivables; it does not create a second sale or deduct stock
again.
Bagsakan POS + Inventory | Developer Specification 6
9. Spoilage, expenses and dashboards
Spoilage / wastage: Record SKU, batch where applicable, warehouse, quantity, date, reason,
optional photo, submitter and approver. Approved spoilage reduces stock and records the cost
as a loss.
Example: Tomato stock 100 kg; spoiled 8 kg due to overripeness; Admin approves; new stock 92
kg. The 8 kg remains traceable as a loss.
Expenses: Record category, date, amount, payee, payment method/account and supporting
reference. Track expenses separately from product gross profit and supplier purchase balances.
10. Admin dashboard and reports
Illustrative dashboard metric Amount / count
Sales / COGS / gross profit today ₱125,500 / ₱82,300 / ₱43,200
Inventory value ₱450,000
Receivables / supplier payables ₱185,000 / ₱120,000
Low stock / out of stock 12 / 4 items
Spoilage today ₱3,200
Show top-selling products, low stock, highest gross-profit products, slow-moving products,
spoilage and outstanding customer credits. Use actual sale prices and posted costs for realized
gross profit. Net profit requires operating expenses and other applicable adjustments.
Reports must support date, SKU, supplier, customer and warehouse filters. Include sales, COGS,
gross profit, stock movements, inventory valuation, expenses, supplier purchases/payables and
receivable aging. Exports follow the same role permissions.
11. Supplier profiles
Supplier fields: name, contact person, mobile number, address, TIN, payment terms, bank
information, supplied products and outstanding balance. Reports should answer: “How much
tomato did we buy from Supplier A this month?” and “Which products came from Supplier B
between October 1 and 31?”
Bagsakan POS + Inventory | Developer Specification 7
12. Audit trail and recommended modules
Trace all sensitive changes: pricing, inventory adjustments, spoilage approvals, discounts, voids,
refunds, payments and role changes. Record user, timestamp, source record, before/after values
and reason. Posted records should be reversed with linked entries rather than erased.
Examples: Admin changed tomato SRP ₱90 → ₱95; Cashier 02 voided POS-1055 for ₱1,250 with
approval; Inventory Staff submitted a -5 kg spoilage adjustment.
Module Purpose
Dashboard / POS Business overview; retail and wholesale checkout
Products / Pricing SKU masterfile, units, SRP and price history
Purchasing / Receiving Supplier orders, purchases and receipt batches
Inventory / Spoilage Stock by location, movements, adjustments and losses
Suppliers / Customers Contact records and transaction histories
Credit / AR / Expenses Receivables, collections and business expenses
Reports / Users / Audit trail Financial and inventory reports; permissions; traceability
13. Database structure and acceptance checks
Recommended records: Product; Supplier; Product-Supplier link; Purchase and PurchaseLine;
Receipt and ReceiptLine/Batch; Warehouse; InventoryMovement; SellingPriceHistory; Sale and
SaleLine; Payment and InvoiceAllocation; Expense; Adjustment; User/Role/Permission; AuditLog.
Each purchase line points to one SKU and its purchase supplier. Each receiving batch links back
to a receipt/purchase line. Sale lines retain the selling price, unit conversion and costing applied
when posted.
Developer acceptance checks:
1. Three suppliers for one SKU retain all three costs and total stock.
2. Purchasing users can be blocked from selling prices and profit.
3. Cashier APIs, exports and printouts contain no purchase costs.
4. Tray/pcs and fractional-kg sales deduct correct base quantities.
5. Duplicate checkout/receipt requests cannot post twice; simultaneous sales cannot oversell.
6. Price updates preserve existing invoice prices and history.
7. Collections reduce AR without duplicating sales; spoilage and voids create traceable
movements.
Critical requirement: ONE SKU per product, MULTIPLE SUPPLIERS, MULTIPLE PURCHASE PRICES
and MULTIPLE BATCHES. Protect buying costs, valuation and financial information through
backend permissions.